David Damerell wrote:

> But item identification? Roguelikes don't have to have that, and indeed I
> think some new attempts are damaged by adding it in unthinkingly. Kornel's
> DoomRL doesn't have item ID and I say it's a roguelike.

That may be an interesting game, and it may have RL in the title, but
if it doesn't have the item ID system then the game lacks that which
gave Rogue most of its strategic depth.  That is my basis for
emphasizing the importance of the item ID system.

All the major, current roguelikes have it, yet most games that would
like to be roguelike but fall short (most notably Diablo) don't use
item description scrambling.  That makes it a useful litmus test.

I do know of one rogue-ish game that I personally like that doesn't
have item identification, or indeed any items at all.  ChessRogue is
fairly awesome in my experience.

